1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,844
The geometry of information. A family of distributions is a space, and this video builds

2
00:00:05,844 --> 00:00:11,137
its natural ruler, the Fisher metric, from one question: how hard is it to tell

3
00:00:11,137 --> 00:00:16,431
two distributions apart? Then it follows that ruler to a sphere, a hyperbolic plane, the

4
00:00:16,431 --> 00:00:19,254
natural gradient and two kinds of straight lines.

5
00:00:23,550 --> 00:00:28,899
Start with three families. A coin with bias p: one number, so the family is

6
00:00:28,899 --> 00:00:34,247
an interval. A three-sided die: three probabilities that sum to one, a triangle. A Gaussian

7
00:00:34,247 --> 00:00:39,596
with mean mu and width sigma: a half-plane. Every point of these shapes is a

8
00:00:39,596 --> 00:00:40,665
whole probability distribution.

9
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,474
Move a point and the distribution moves with it. The coordinates are convenient labels, nothing

10
00:00:49,474 --> 00:00:54,148
more. We could label the same coins by their log-odds instead, and the interval would

11
00:00:54,148 --> 00:00:55,706
stretch to the whole line.

12
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:08,501
That is the problem. Two coins five hundredths apart in p are two tenths apart

13
00:01:08,501 --> 00:01:13,452
in log-odds near the middle, and one point three apart near the edge. Coordinates do

14
00:01:13,452 --> 00:01:18,072
not measure anything. A distance between distributions has to come from the distributions themselves.

15
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:30,137
Information geometry's answer is a ruler built from distinguishability: how well can data tell two

16
00:01:30,137 --> 00:01:35,475
nearby distributions apart? That ruler is the Fisher metric, and the rest of this video

17
00:01:35,475 --> 00:01:36,186
follows it.

18
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:46,433
Before comparing coins, watch where these count distributions come from. Take a coin with p

19
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:51,815
equal to one half and flip it a hundred times: gold for heads, grey for

20
00:01:51,815 --> 00:01:57,198
tails. Count the heads: here, forty-three. Do it again: forty-four. And again: forty-eight. Each run

21
00:01:57,198 --> 00:02:02,580
of a hundred flips gives one number, and the numbers differ, because the flips are

22
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:02,939
random.

23
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:19,378
Repeat the run two thousand times and tally the counts. The tally settles into a

24
00:02:19,378 --> 00:02:24,107
hill: most runs give close to fifty heads, a run with forty or sixty is

25
00:02:24,107 --> 00:02:28,835
rare, thirty almost never happens. The exact shape is the binomial distribution, drawn as the

26
00:02:28,835 --> 00:02:32,618
curve, and for a hundred flips it is close to a Gaussian.

27
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:46,664
What sets the width of the hill? One flip is a yes-or-no with mean p

28
00:02:46,664 --> 00:02:51,079
and variance p times one minus p. A hundred independent flips add their variances, so

29
00:02:51,079 --> 00:02:55,493
the count has variance a hundred times p times one minus p, and its standard

30
00:02:55,493 --> 00:02:59,907
deviation is the square root of that. At p equal one half, that is five

31
00:02:59,907 --> 00:03:00,201
heads.

32
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:16,784
Near p equal to zero the coin almost always shows tails, so the count hardly

33
00:03:16,784 --> 00:03:21,318
varies: at p equal point oh two the standard deviation is one point four heads.

34
00:03:21,318 --> 00:03:25,851
A fair coin is the most unpredictable coin there is; a lopsided coin is nearly

35
00:03:25,851 --> 00:03:30,385
deterministic. The spread is a property of the coin, not of the scale of the

36
00:03:30,385 --> 00:03:33,408
picture: here both hills are drawn on the same axis.

37
00:03:42,250 --> 00:03:45,904
Here is the whole family on one axis. As p runs from zero to one,

38
00:03:45,904 --> 00:03:49,558
the hill slides from the left edge to the right, narrow at both ends and

39
00:03:49,558 --> 00:03:53,212
widest in the middle. Keep this in mind for the next scene: a step in

40
00:03:53,212 --> 00:03:56,866
p moves the hill by the same number of heads everywhere, but the hills it

41
00:03:56,866 --> 00:04:00,032
has to clear are wide in the middle and narrow at the edges.

42
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:20,015
Now compare coins. Two coins near the middle, p equal point five and point five

43
00:04:20,015 --> 00:04:25,380
five, a hundred flips each. On the same axis as before, here are their two

44
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:30,745
count hills: both wide, five heads apart, overlapping almost entirely. A hundred flips can barely

45
00:04:30,745 --> 00:04:32,176
tell these coins apart.

46
00:04:39,950 --> 00:04:44,957
Two coins near the edge, p equal point oh two and point oh seven. The

47
00:04:44,957 --> 00:04:49,964
same step of five hundredths, the same hundred flips, the same axis. These hills are

48
00:04:49,964 --> 00:04:54,970
narrow, so the same five heads of separation leaves them almost disjoint: two heads against

49
00:04:54,970 --> 00:04:59,977
seven, visible at a glance. The two zooms below show each pair in detail, each

50
00:04:59,977 --> 00:05:01,312
at its own magnification.

51
00:05:09,650 --> 00:05:14,134
Measure the step in units of the hills' own width, the standard deviation. Near the

52
00:05:14,134 --> 00:05:18,618
middle the width is five heads, so the step is one standard deviation. Near the

53
00:05:18,618 --> 00:05:23,102
edge the width is about two heads, so the same step is about two and

54
00:05:23,102 --> 00:05:27,586
a half. The same coordinate step buys very different amounts of evidence, and the exchange

55
00:05:27,586 --> 00:05:29,379
rate depends on where you stand.

56
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:43,692
That exchange rate is the Fisher information. Divide the step in heads by the width

57
00:05:43,692 --> 00:05:48,034
of the hill: n delta p over root n p one minus p, which is

58
00:05:48,034 --> 00:05:52,377
root n times delta p over root p one minus p. The factor that depends

59
00:05:52,377 --> 00:05:56,719
on the coin, one over p one minus p, is g of p: four in

60
00:05:56,719 --> 00:06:01,061
the middle, fifty-one near the edge. It is the local ruler of the family. The

61
00:06:01,061 --> 00:06:05,403
number of standard deviations between two nearby coins after n flips is root n g

62
00:06:05,403 --> 00:06:06,272
times delta p.

63
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:23,626
A ruler that changes from place to place is exactly what a Riemannian metric is.

64
00:06:23,626 --> 00:06:25,808
Next: what this ruler is made of.

65
00:06:29,950 --> 00:06:34,663
Now take the ruler apart. A statistical model is a table: for every value of

66
00:06:34,663 --> 00:06:39,375
the parameter theta, one probability distribution over the outcomes x. Write it p of x

67
00:06:39,375 --> 00:06:44,088
given theta: the probability of seeing x if the coin is theta. For one flip

68
00:06:44,088 --> 00:06:48,801
the outcomes are heads and tails; p of heads given theta is theta, and p

69
00:06:48,801 --> 00:06:53,513
of tails given theta is one minus theta. Slide theta and the two probabilities change.

70
00:07:02,350 --> 00:07:06,968
For a hundred flips the outcome is the number of heads, and p of k

71
00:07:06,968 --> 00:07:11,585
given theta is the binomial hill of the previous scene, centred at a hundred theta.

72
00:07:11,585 --> 00:07:14,971
One parameter value, one hill; slide theta and the hill slides.

73
00:07:21,550 --> 00:07:26,531
Now turn the table around. Fix what you saw and ask how probable it was

74
00:07:26,531 --> 00:07:31,511
under each theta. That function of theta is the likelihood. You saw heads: the likelihood

75
00:07:31,511 --> 00:07:36,492
is theta itself, a straight line rising to one. You saw tails: one minus theta,

76
00:07:36,492 --> 00:07:36,824
falling.

77
00:07:41,950 --> 00:07:46,206
Take logarithms. The log-likelihood of heads is log theta, and its slope at theta equal

78
00:07:46,206 --> 00:07:50,462
p is one over p: that is the score of heads, s of H at

79
00:07:50,462 --> 00:07:54,717
p. For tails the slope of log one minus theta is minus one over one

80
00:07:54,717 --> 00:07:58,973
minus p: the score of tails. H and T are nothing more than the two

81
00:07:58,973 --> 00:08:03,229
outcomes. A score is a slope: how fast the log-probability of what you saw changes

82
00:08:03,229 --> 00:08:04,647
as you move the parameter.

83
00:08:22,750 --> 00:08:27,363
Why the logarithm? The slope of log p is the change of p divided by

84
00:08:27,363 --> 00:08:31,977
p: the relative change. That is what evidence is made of. One more percentage point

85
00:08:31,977 --> 00:08:36,590
of probability is a small matter when p is one half, and a doubling when

86
00:08:36,590 --> 00:08:41,204
p is one in a hundred. Second, independent observations multiply their probabilities, so their logarithms

87
00:08:41,204 --> 00:08:45,817
add, and the score of a hundred flips is simply the sum of a hundred

88
00:08:45,817 --> 00:08:50,431
single-flip scores: k over p minus n minus k over one minus p. Each flip

89
00:08:50,431 --> 00:08:52,276
votes, and the votes add up.

90
00:09:08,350 --> 00:09:13,487
Third, the Gaussian. Its log-density is a parabola, minus x minus mu squared over two

91
00:09:13,487 --> 00:09:18,623
sigma squared, so the score in mu is x minus mu over sigma squared: the

92
00:09:18,623 --> 00:09:23,760
observation's distance from the mean, in units of the variance. The logarithm turns the bell

93
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:26,499
into the parabola whose curvature is the information.

94
00:09:34,750 --> 00:09:39,494
One more property. At the true coin the average score is zero. Heads pushes up

95
00:09:39,494 --> 00:09:44,238
by one over p, and happens with probability p; tails pushes down by one over

96
00:09:44,238 --> 00:09:48,982
one minus p, with probability one minus p. The two pushes cancel exactly. That is

97
00:09:48,982 --> 00:09:53,726
no accident: the probabilities of all outcomes sum to one for every theta, so their

98
00:09:53,726 --> 00:09:58,470
derivatives sum to zero. On average the data push neither way, but they do push,

99
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:01,316
and how hard they push is the next scene.

100
00:10:13,150 --> 00:10:17,892
The score of one observation pushes theta up or down, and at the true coin

101
00:10:17,892 --> 00:10:22,634
the pushes average to zero. The Fisher information measures how hard they push: the mean

102
00:10:22,634 --> 00:10:27,376
of the squared score, which is its variance. Take the coin p equal point three.

103
00:10:27,376 --> 00:10:32,118
Heads contributes one over p squared, with probability p; tails one over one minus p

104
00:10:32,118 --> 00:10:36,860
squared, with probability one minus p. Add the two: one over p plus one over

105
00:10:36,860 --> 00:10:41,601
one minus p, which is one over p times one minus p. Four point seven

106
00:10:41,601 --> 00:10:46,343
six here, four at p equal one half, fifty-one at p equal point oh two.

107
00:10:58,650 --> 00:11:02,939
Scores of independent flips add, and the variance of a sum of independent terms is

108
00:11:02,939 --> 00:11:07,228
the sum of the variances, so a hundred flips carry a hundred times the information

109
00:11:07,228 --> 00:11:11,518
of one. That is the root n g of the earlier scene: the information of

110
00:11:11,518 --> 00:11:15,807
n flips is n times g, and the number of standard deviations in a step

111
00:11:15,807 --> 00:11:17,809
is its square root times delta p.

112
00:11:36,350 --> 00:11:41,696
Second form. Fix the true coin, p zero equal point three, and ask how good

113
00:11:41,696 --> 00:11:47,042
each candidate theta is on average. For each theta take the log-likelihood of heads and

114
00:11:47,042 --> 00:11:52,389
of tails, weight them by the true probabilities, point three and point seven, and average.

115
00:11:52,389 --> 00:11:57,379
As theta sweeps across, the weighted average traces the lavender curve: the expected log-likelihood.

116
00:12:08,850 --> 00:12:13,590
The curve peaks at the truth: on average no other coin explains the data better

117
00:12:13,590 --> 00:12:18,331
than the true one. Its slope at the peak is the mean score, zero. Its

118
00:12:18,331 --> 00:12:23,071
curvature at the peak is minus the Fisher information. Here is why. The mean score

119
00:12:23,071 --> 00:12:27,811
is zero for every theta; differentiate that statement with respect to theta. Two terms appear:

120
00:12:27,811 --> 00:12:32,551
the derivative of the probabilities times the score, which is the squared score, and the

121
00:12:32,551 --> 00:12:37,292
probabilities times the derivative of the score. Their sum is zero, so the variance of

122
00:12:37,292 --> 00:12:42,032
the score equals minus the expected second derivative of the log-likelihood. A sharp peak pins

123
00:12:42,032 --> 00:12:44,876
the parameter down; a flat one leaves it loose.

124
00:13:00,850 --> 00:13:05,892
Third form. Flip the curve. The gap between the expected log-likelihood at the truth and

125
00:13:05,892 --> 00:13:10,934
at theta is the Kullback–Leibler divergence from p zero to theta: the average log-likelihood ratio

126
00:13:10,934 --> 00:13:15,977
per observation, how much better the truth explains its own data than theta does. It

127
00:13:15,977 --> 00:13:21,019
is zero at the truth, flat there, and rises like one half g times theta

128
00:13:21,019 --> 00:13:26,061
minus p zero squared. The Fisher information is the curvature of KL at coincidence, and

129
00:13:26,061 --> 00:13:28,750
every f-divergence shares it up to a constant.

130
00:13:38,550 --> 00:13:43,201
Three forms of one quantity: the variance of the score, the expected curvature of the

131
00:13:43,201 --> 00:13:47,851
log-likelihood, and the quadratic part of KL. All three were computed in the coordinate p.

132
00:13:47,851 --> 00:13:52,502
Change coordinates, to the log-odds say, and the score changes like a slope does, so

133
00:13:52,502 --> 00:13:57,152
the variance changes by the square of the conversion factor. What does not change is

134
00:13:57,152 --> 00:14:01,493
the length of a path measured with it, and that is the next scene.

135
00:14:12,350 --> 00:14:16,721
Fisher information is not a number attached to a distribution; it depends on the coordinates.

136
00:14:16,721 --> 00:14:21,092
Change from p to the log-odds x and it changes by the square of dp

137
00:14:21,092 --> 00:14:25,464
by dx, as a metric tensor must. What does not change is the length of

138
00:14:25,464 --> 00:14:29,835
a path, the integral of root g along it: from p equal point one to

139
00:14:29,835 --> 00:14:33,040
point seven it is one point three four in either coordinate.

140
00:14:42,950 --> 00:14:47,694
For the coin there is a coordinate in which the ruler is constant. Take phi

141
00:14:47,694 --> 00:14:52,439
equal to two arcsine root p. Then d phi squared is g d p squared:

142
00:14:52,439 --> 00:14:57,183
in phi, equally distinguishable coins are equally spaced, and the whole family, from p zero

143
00:14:57,183 --> 00:14:58,764
to one, has length pi.

144
00:15:08,450 --> 00:15:12,726
Here is why. Send the coin to the point two root p, two root one

145
00:15:12,726 --> 00:15:17,003
minus p, on the circle of radius two. The Fisher metric is the arc length

146
00:15:17,003 --> 00:15:21,279
of that circle, and the Fisher–Rao distance between two coins is an angle: two arccos

147
00:15:21,279 --> 00:15:24,700
of root p q plus root one minus p one minus q.

148
00:15:33,950 --> 00:15:39,232
Now count. After n flips, coins one standard deviation apart sit one over root n

149
00:15:39,232 --> 00:15:44,514
apart in phi. So a hundred flips can tell apart about thirty-one coins along the

150
00:15:44,514 --> 00:15:49,797
whole family, and in p they crowd towards zero and one. That is Wootters' reading

151
00:15:49,797 --> 00:15:52,614
of the metric: Fisher–Rao distance counts distinguishable distributions.

152
00:16:01,150 --> 00:16:05,616
Keep this picture. The Fisher metric is the arc length of a sphere in disguise,

153
00:16:05,616 --> 00:16:07,998
and with three outcomes the disguise comes off.

154
00:16:14,750 --> 00:16:18,958
Three outcomes. The dice fill a triangle, and the Fisher metric at each point is

155
00:16:18,958 --> 00:16:23,166
the sum over outcomes of d p i squared over p i. Here are balls

156
00:16:23,166 --> 00:16:27,374
of one Fisher radius at different places: round in the middle, flattened against the edges

157
00:16:27,374 --> 00:16:31,582
and crushed into the corners, where a small change of a rare outcome is a

158
00:16:31,582 --> 00:16:32,143
big step.

159
00:16:41,950 --> 00:16:46,817
Now stretch each coordinate to twice its square root. The triangle bends into the positive

160
00:16:46,817 --> 00:16:51,683
eighth of a sphere of radius two, and in these coordinates the Fisher metric is

161
00:16:51,683 --> 00:16:56,225
the ordinary round metric. The flattened balls become round caps, all of one size.

162
00:17:09,150 --> 00:17:14,021
So the geometry of three-sided dice is spherical. Geodesics are great circles. The Fisher–Rao distance

163
00:17:14,021 --> 00:17:18,891
between two dice is two arccos of the sum of root p i q i,

164
00:17:18,891 --> 00:17:23,762
an angle again, never more than pi. And the straight chord between the same two

165
00:17:23,762 --> 00:17:26,684
points is, up to a constant, the Hellinger distance.

166
00:17:36,350 --> 00:17:40,918
Back in the triangle the geodesic bows toward the centre; the straight segment between the

167
00:17:40,918 --> 00:17:45,487
two dice is longer. The straight segment is not wrong, though: it is the mixture

168
00:17:45,487 --> 00:17:50,055
of the two dice, a geodesic of a different, flat geometry that returns at the

169
00:17:50,055 --> 00:17:51,273
end of the video.

170
00:18:00,350 --> 00:18:05,870
One picture to keep: probabilities live on a sphere, and distinguishability is the angle between

171
00:18:05,870 --> 00:18:06,238
them.

172
00:18:19,550 --> 00:18:24,787
Why this ruler and not another? Here are two dice on the triangle, zero point

173
00:18:24,787 --> 00:18:30,025
nine seven apart. Relabel the outcomes, turning the triangle onto itself, and nothing changes: the

174
00:18:30,025 --> 00:18:35,262
metric does not care which face is called one. Now merge outcomes two and three:

175
00:18:35,262 --> 00:18:39,452
each die becomes a coin, and the triangle collapses onto one edge.

176
00:18:47,450 --> 00:18:52,334
The two coins are zero point one one apart. Merging threw away exactly the information

177
00:18:52,334 --> 00:18:57,219
that told these dice apart, and the distance shrank with it. In general a statistic

178
00:18:57,219 --> 00:19:01,777
can only shrink Fisher–Rao distances, and it keeps them exactly when it is sufficient.

179
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:16,080
Go the other way: split outcome three into two sub-outcomes in fixed proportions. The dice

180
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:21,461
gain a face but no information, and the distance is unchanged: zero point nine seven

181
00:19:21,461 --> 00:19:26,841
again. Chentsov's theorem, from 1972, says that a metric defined on all the simplices and

182
00:19:26,841 --> 00:19:32,222
invariant under every such splitting is a constant multiple of the Fisher metric. There is

183
00:19:32,222 --> 00:19:33,657
no other invariant ruler.

184
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:44,828
The theorem extends to general sample spaces and to densities on manifolds, and it has

185
00:19:44,828 --> 00:19:49,506
a twin for connections that we meet at the end. The Fisher metric is not

186
00:19:49,506 --> 00:19:54,183
a convention. It is forced by asking that distances between distributions mean the same thing

187
00:19:54,183 --> 00:19:55,743
whatever we call the outcomes.

188
00:20:01,850 --> 00:20:06,778
Gaussians, with mean mu and width sigma. The Fisher metric is one over sigma squared

189
00:20:06,778 --> 00:20:11,705
along mu and two over sigma squared along sigma: both shrink as the width grows.

190
00:20:11,705 --> 00:20:16,633
Here are Fisher circles of one size across the half-plane. Near the bottom, where distributions

191
00:20:16,633 --> 00:20:21,561
are narrow, a tiny shift is a big step; high up, wide Gaussians barely notice

192
00:20:21,561 --> 00:20:22,547
the same shift.

193
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:34,591
Rescale mu by root two and this is the Poincaré half-plane, up to a factor

194
00:20:34,591 --> 00:20:39,782
two: a surface of constant negative curvature, minus one half, as Hotelling noticed as early

195
00:20:39,782 --> 00:20:44,974
as 1929. Geodesics are the half-ellipses that stand on the mu axis, and the distance

196
00:20:44,974 --> 00:20:46,358
has a closed form.

197
00:20:52,600 --> 00:20:57,427
So the shortest path between two narrow Gaussians far apart is not the straight segment.

198
00:20:57,427 --> 00:21:02,255
It climbs: it widens the distribution, slides it across while it is wide and cheap

199
00:21:02,255 --> 00:21:06,117
to move, and narrows it again. The bells below trace that path.

200
00:21:14,350 --> 00:21:19,374
Widen the second Gaussian and the distance drops; move its mean and the geodesic bends

201
00:21:19,374 --> 00:21:24,397
to follow. The two sliders under the video are yours at any time: this scene

202
00:21:24,397 --> 00:21:29,421
is computed live. And note the contrast with optimal transport: in the Wasserstein geometry this

203
00:21:29,421 --> 00:21:34,445
half-plane is flat and the straight segment wins. Fisher–Rao pays for distinguishability, not for how

204
00:21:34,445 --> 00:21:35,784
far the mass moves.

205
00:21:43,350 --> 00:21:48,305
Unlike the sphere of the simplex, this plane is unbounded: two unit Gaussians a hundred

206
00:21:48,305 --> 00:21:53,261
apart are about twelve units apart, and the distance grows only logarithmically with the gap.

207
00:22:06,550 --> 00:22:11,778
What does the ruler buy? First, resolution. Estimate a die from a hundred rolls, many

208
00:22:11,778 --> 00:22:17,006
times over, and the estimates scatter around the truth. The Cramér–Rao bound says no unbiased

209
00:22:17,006 --> 00:22:22,233
estimator scatters less than the inverse Fisher metric divided by n, and the sample frequencies

210
00:22:22,233 --> 00:22:27,461
attain it. The one-sigma cloud of estimates is exactly the Fisher ball of radius one

211
00:22:27,461 --> 00:22:28,507
over root n.

212
00:22:37,550 --> 00:22:42,772
So the balls of the earlier scenes were pictures of estimation error: large where distributions

213
00:22:42,772 --> 00:22:47,993
are hard to tell apart, small where they are easy. The bound is an inequality

214
00:22:47,993 --> 00:22:53,215
between tensors: change coordinates and both sides transform together, which is why it holds in

215
00:22:53,215 --> 00:22:53,911
every parametrisation.

216
00:23:02,350 --> 00:23:06,666
Second, a prior. Jeffreys asked for a prior that does not depend on the parametrisation,

217
00:23:06,666 --> 00:23:10,983
and the answer is the Riemannian volume of the Fisher metric, root det g. For

218
00:23:10,983 --> 00:23:15,299
the coin it is one over pi root p one minus p: piled up near

219
00:23:15,299 --> 00:23:19,615
zero and one in p, flat in the angle phi. For the die it is

220
00:23:19,615 --> 00:23:20,766
uniform on the sphere.

221
00:23:30,250 --> 00:23:34,799
Resolution and volume are the metric read two ways. A ball of Fisher radius one

222
00:23:34,799 --> 00:23:39,348
over root n is what n observations can resolve, and the number of such balls

223
00:23:39,348 --> 00:23:43,897
that fit in a model is its Jeffreys volume: the complexity term of minimum description

224
00:23:43,897 --> 00:23:44,201
length.

225
00:23:55,050 --> 00:23:59,842
Now use the ruler to learn. Fit a Gaussian to a target by minimising the

226
00:23:59,842 --> 00:24:04,634
KL divergence. Plain gradient descent asks which step of fixed coordinate length lowers the loss

227
00:24:04,634 --> 00:24:09,427
most. But coordinate length means nothing here, and the plain gradient in mu carries a

228
00:24:09,427 --> 00:24:14,219
factor one over sigma squared: huge when the fit is narrow, tiny when it is

229
00:24:14,219 --> 00:24:14,538
wide.

230
00:24:21,150 --> 00:24:26,097
Ask instead for the step of fixed Fisher length, and the answer is the inverse

231
00:24:26,097 --> 00:24:31,044
metric times the gradient: Amari's natural gradient. It is the same vector in every parametrisation,

232
00:24:31,044 --> 00:24:35,991
and for this loss its mu component is simply mu minus mu star, whatever sigma

233
00:24:35,991 --> 00:24:36,321
is.

234
00:24:42,900 --> 00:24:47,814
Start narrow and far from the target. Plain descent with step size point three overshoots

235
00:24:47,814 --> 00:24:52,728
at once: sigma jumps to twenty-four, mu to minus point six, and the fit never

236
00:24:52,728 --> 00:24:57,642
recovers. With step size point oh two it is safe, and after sixty steps it

237
00:24:57,642 --> 00:25:01,246
is still nowhere near. No single step size serves both regimes.

238
00:25:07,550 --> 00:25:12,949
The natural gradient with step size point three widens first, slides across while wide, and

239
00:25:12,949 --> 00:25:18,348
narrows onto the target in about fifteen steps: the geodesic's strategy, found without knowing the

240
00:25:18,348 --> 00:25:23,746
geodesic. In the limit of small steps its path is a straight line in the

241
00:25:23,746 --> 00:25:29,145
expectation coordinates, mu and mu squared plus sigma squared: a geodesic of the second, flat

242
00:25:29,145 --> 00:25:29,505
geometry.

243
00:25:36,550 --> 00:25:41,928
Steepest descent means nothing without a metric. With the Fisher metric the descent happens in

244
00:25:41,928 --> 00:25:45,155
the space of distributions, and the parametrisation drops out.

245
00:25:59,750 --> 00:26:04,822
There is more structure than the metric. On the simplex two other kinds of line

246
00:26:04,822 --> 00:26:09,895
are natural. Mixtures: the straight segment from p to q, with weights one minus s

247
00:26:09,895 --> 00:26:14,967
and s. Exponential arcs: straight in log probabilities, the geometric mixtures p to the one

248
00:26:14,967 --> 00:26:20,040
minus s times q to the s, renormalised. And between them the Fisher–Rao geodesic, the

249
00:26:20,040 --> 00:26:20,716
great circle.

250
00:26:29,450 --> 00:26:34,674
Each kind is straight in its own chart. Write the die as an exponential family:

251
00:26:34,674 --> 00:26:39,898
natural parameters theta, the log-odds against the third face, and log-partition psi. In the theta

252
00:26:39,898 --> 00:26:45,122
plane the exponential arcs are straight and the mixtures bend. In the probability triangle, the

253
00:26:45,122 --> 00:26:48,256
mean parameters eta, it is the other way round.

254
00:26:59,150 --> 00:27:04,136
The two charts are Legendre dual. Eta is the gradient of psi; theta is the

255
00:27:04,136 --> 00:27:09,121
gradient of the dual potential phi, which is minus the entropy; and the Fisher metric

256
00:27:09,121 --> 00:27:14,107
is the Hessian of either, g in theta, its inverse in eta. That is why,

257
00:27:14,107 --> 00:27:18,760
for an exponential family, the Fisher metric is the covariance of the sufficient statistic.

258
00:27:28,850 --> 00:27:33,893
Amari's reading: the two kinds of straight line are geodesics of two flat connections, e

259
00:27:33,893 --> 00:27:38,935
and m, dual with respect to the Fisher metric: the derivative of an inner product

260
00:27:38,935 --> 00:27:43,978
splits into an e-derivative of one factor and an m-derivative of the other. Their average

261
00:27:43,978 --> 00:27:49,020
is the Levi-Civita connection of the sphere, which is curved. That is a dually flat

262
00:27:49,020 --> 00:27:54,063
space, and Chentsov's twin theorem makes these connections, with the alpha-family between them, the only

263
00:27:54,063 --> 00:27:54,735
invariant ones.

264
00:28:01,850 --> 00:28:07,225
Three kinds of straight line, one metric, and a duality between two charts. The payoff

265
00:28:07,225 --> 00:28:09,375
is a Pythagorean theorem for KL.

266
00:28:18,350 --> 00:28:23,125
Legendre duality gives a divergence for free: the Bregman divergence of psi, the gap between

267
00:28:23,125 --> 00:28:27,899
psi and its tangent plane. For an exponential family it is the KL divergence with

268
00:28:27,899 --> 00:28:32,674
the arguments swapped. It is not symmetric and not a distance, but to second order

269
00:28:32,674 --> 00:28:36,176
it is one half of the Fisher metric, as we saw.

270
00:28:44,750 --> 00:28:48,926
And it obeys a Pythagorean theorem. Take a mixture segment from p to q and

271
00:28:48,926 --> 00:28:53,103
an exponential arc from q to r that meet at a right angle at q,

272
00:28:53,103 --> 00:28:57,279
measured by the Fisher metric. Then KL of p from r equals KL of p

273
00:28:57,279 --> 00:29:01,455
from q plus KL of q from r. With numbers: point one two four plus

274
00:29:01,455 --> 00:29:03,961
point two four zero equals point three six four.

275
00:29:14,450 --> 00:29:19,820
The consequence is a projection theorem. Extend the exponential arc into a whole e-flat line

276
00:29:19,820 --> 00:29:25,190
and plot KL from p along it: it is minimised exactly at the foot q

277
00:29:25,190 --> 00:29:30,561
of the perpendicular. Minimising KL from a fixed p over an exponential family is an

278
00:29:30,561 --> 00:29:35,931
m-projection, unique by convexity. Maximum likelihood is this projection, of the empirical distribution onto the

279
00:29:35,931 --> 00:29:36,289
model.

280
00:29:44,150 --> 00:29:49,823
The EM algorithm is two such projections alternating: an e-projection of the current model onto

281
00:29:49,823 --> 00:29:55,497
the data manifold, which computes the posterior over the hidden variables, then an m-projection back

282
00:29:55,497 --> 00:30:01,170
onto the model, which maximises the complete-data likelihood. Each step is the foot of a

283
00:30:01,170 --> 00:30:03,061
perpendicular, and KL never increases.

284
00:30:07,250 --> 00:30:11,061
So the geometry has two flat charts and a Pythagoras between them.

285
00:30:20,450 --> 00:30:25,976
So, end to end. Distances between distributions come from distinguishability, and the local exchange rate

286
00:30:25,976 --> 00:30:31,501
between a parameter step and evidence is the Fisher metric: the variance of the score,

287
00:30:31,501 --> 00:30:35,185
the curvature of the likelihood, the quadratic part of KL.

288
00:30:38,450 --> 00:30:42,975
On the simplex that metric is the round metric of a sphere, reached by taking

289
00:30:42,975 --> 00:30:46,897
square roots, so distance is an angle and the Jeffreys prior is uniform.

290
00:30:53,450 --> 00:30:58,697
On the Gaussians it is a hyperbolic plane, where geodesics widen before they move; the

291
00:30:58,697 --> 00:31:02,895
same metric sets the resolution of estimation and defines the natural gradient.

292
00:31:09,950 --> 00:31:15,418
It is the only metric that is invariant under relabelling and never inflated by coarse-graining:

293
00:31:15,418 --> 00:31:16,147
Chentsov's theorem.

294
00:31:23,450 --> 00:31:29,644
And beneath it lie two flat charts, natural and expectation parameters, Legendre dual, whose straight

295
00:31:29,644 --> 00:31:35,838
lines meet at right angles in a Pythagorean theorem for KL. Distributions as points, distinguishability

296
00:31:35,838 --> 00:31:39,142
as distance: that is the geometry of information.
